`stdio.h` – Provides standard input and output functions.
`stdlib.h` – Contains functions for memory allocation and conversion.
`unistd.h` – Provides system-related functions such as `sleep()`.
`argc` – Represents the total number of command-line arguments.
`argv[]` – Stores the arguments supplied to the program.
`char *ptr` – Declares a pointer that holds a memory address.
`atoi()` – Converts a string value into an integer.
`malloc()` – Dynamically allocates memory during program execution.
`NULL` – Represents a null pointer and is used to check allocation failure.
`1024 * 1024` – Converts megabytes into bytes.
`for` – Repeats a block of statements.
`ptr[i] = 'A'` – Stores the character A in the allocated memory.
`while(1)` – Creates an infinite loop.
`sleep(1)` – Pauses program execution for one second.
`free()` – Releases dynamically allocated memory.
`gcc` – Compiles C source code into an executable program.
`free -h` – Displays memory usage in human-readable format.
`watch -n 1` – Refreshes command output after every one second.
`Ctrl + C` – Terminates the currently running program.
